  "text": "You're a casino owner and you have a problem. People don't like losing their money to you. Here's how you fix it. Take down all the clocks, block out the windows and rearrange your place to have a maze-like layout. Now their sense of time and direction starts to blur. But this is worth nothing unless you do the critical step of replacing money with chips. It is so much harder to keep track of how much you're down when it's just some abstract tokens. But people still want to leave. Before they do, give them free drinks. Now they feel valued. Plus, this stuff isn't really known for improving risk management. Switch to warm lighting, play some music, and now it's cozy. But some of them are starting to lose big time and want to get out before it's too much. Stop them right there and hand them their new VIP loyalty card. They are now premium customers and are eligible for free dinners, hotels, and exclusive offers. And all they had to do is lose their entire paycheck. But reassure them, they're not losing, they're earning loyalty points. Uh-oh, some high roller just dropped six figures and looks devastated. Reward the biggest losers the most. Private host, luxury suite, and even give him back a tiny portion of his loss. You're his friend through his toughest moment. Not someone who strategically calculates the smallest gift needed to keep him gambling. And there you have it. A happy casino. Remember, you don't have to beat them. You just need to make sure they enjoy losing them."
}
